---
title: One state, one name
date: 2026-08-02
summary: A rejected application used to read as "Rejected" in the panel, sit under a column called "Closed", and be counted in a bucket named "rejected" — three names for one thing. Now there is one, and your mail explains what it did to the stage.
type: changelog
tags:
  - job-seekers
  - tracking
---

An application has one state. The tracker had three names for it.

Open a rejected application and the panel said **Rejected**. On the board it sat under a
column called **Closed**. On the Pipeline tab it was counted in a bucket named
**rejected**, next to two more — *No answer* and *In progress* — that appeared nowhere
else in the product. Nothing was broken, but you had to work out for yourself that all of
it was the same application.

**There is now one vocabulary.** The board's columns, the funnel's bands and the stage
picker all read the same four groups — Applied, Interview, Offer, Closed — and every card
in Closed still carries its own outcome, so you can see at a glance whether those 28
settled applications were rejections or accepted offers. The stage picker groups its
options the same way, so *Closed* reads as a heading over Accepted, Rejected and Withdrawn
rather than as a fifth state competing with them.

The Pipeline tab now shows each band broken down by stage instead of collapsing it. A
`Closed 28` that turns out to be `Rejected 28` is a different month from one that reads
`Accepted 2 · Rejected 26`.

## Your mail now says what it did

The other half of the confusion was quieter. Seven emails on an application, including a
plain rejection, and the stage never moved — with nothing anywhere saying why.

That was deliberate: we advance a stage from mail only forwards, and **never into a
settled outcome**. Deciding an application is over is your call, not something we infer
from a message. But the rule was invisible, so it looked like nothing was working.

Every classified message on an application now says what it means for the stage —
`Received → Applied`, or, on a rejection, plainly *does not move the stage*. And when the
newest message disagrees with where the application sits, you get a one-press offer to
move it. It applies nothing on its own; once you've set the stage yourself, the offer
stops asking.

If you use the API, `GET /me/tracking/pipeline` now returns counts per stage instead of
the seven buckets — the same vocabulary you already set through the tracking endpoint.
